1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,946
What if the gods knew exactly how the world would end—and chose to face it anyway?

2
00:00:02,986 --> 00:00:05,932
The Norse called that ending Ragnarök.

3
00:00:05,972 --> 00:00:08,919
It began with three terrible winters, one after another.

4
00:00:08,959 --> 00:00:11,906
No summer came.

5
00:00:11,946 --> 00:00:14,892
Families turned against each other, and the world grew cold.

6
00:00:14,932 --> 00:00:17,879
Then a wolf swallowed the sun.

7
00:00:17,919 --> 00:00:20,866
Another seized the moon.

8
00:00:20,906 --> 00:00:23,852
The stars vanished, and the roots of Yggdrasil, the world tree, began to shake.

9
00:00:23,892 --> 00:00:26,839
Fenrir, the giant wolf, shattered his chains.

10
00:00:26,879 --> 00:00:32,812
From the ocean rose Jörmungandr, the serpent that encircled the earth, dragging waves across the land.

11
00:00:32,852 --> 00:00:35,799
Heimdall blew his horn.

12
00:00:35,839 --> 00:00:38,786
The gods armed themselves, and Odin rode at their head.

13
00:00:38,826 --> 00:00:41,306
The prophecy had told them how this battle would end.

14
00:00:41,346 --> 00:00:42,146
Odin confronted Fenrir.

15
00:00:41,812 --> 00:00:44,759
The wolf swallowed the king of the gods.

16
00:00:44,799 --> 00:00:47,746
But Odin’s son, Víðarr, stepped forward and tore the beast apart.

17
00:00:47,786 --> 00:00:50,732
Thor met the world serpent amid poisonous waves.

18
00:00:50,772 --> 00:00:53,719
His hammer killed Jörmungandr.

19
00:00:53,759 --> 00:00:56,706
Thor staggered nine steps away—then fell to the serpent’s venom.

20
00:00:56,746 --> 00:00:59,692
And then Surtr came, his sword blazing brighter than the sun.

21
00:00:59,732 --> 00:01:02,679
Fire swept across the battlefield, and the old world sank beneath the sea.

22
00:01:02,719 --> 00:01:04,879
For a moment, nothing remained.

23
00:01:04,919 --> 00:01:05,719
Then the water receded.

24
00:01:05,706 --> 00:01:08,652
Green earth rose again.

25
00:01:08,692 --> 00:01:14,439
The surviving gods returned, and two humans emerged to begin anew.

26
00:01:14,479 --> 00:01:17,612
So Ragnarök was never only a prophecy of destruction.

27
00:01:17,652 --> 00:01:21,410
It was a story of courage in the face of fate—and of life after the end.
